.TH LN 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
ln \- make links between files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ln
.RB [ \-sfnv ]
.I target
.RI [ link ]
.br
.B ln
.RB [ \-sfnv ]
.IR target ...
.I directory
.SH DESCRIPTION
In the first form,
.B ln
makes a link named
.I link
to
.IR target .
With no
.IR link ,
the link is made in the current directory with the target's last name.
If
.I link
is an existing directory, the link goes inside it, as in the second form.
.PP
In the second form,
.B ln
makes a link to each
.I target
inside
.IR directory ,
each named after its target's last name.
.PP
Without
.BR \-s ,
.B ln
makes a hard link, which no filesystem in mcos supports: it always fails
with "Operation not permitted".
.SH OPTIONS
Options must come before the first operand; they may be combined
.RB ( \-sf ).
.TP
.B \-s
Make symbolic links. The target is stored as given; a relative target is
followed from the link's directory, not from where
.B ln
ran.
.TP
.B \-f
Remove an existing file or link of the link's name first.
.TP
.B \-n
Treat a last operand that is a symbolic link to a directory as a name,
not as a directory to put links in (with
.BR \-f ,
it is replaced).
.TP
.B \-v
Print each link made, as
.IR link " \-> " target ,
each name in single quotes.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if every link was made; 1 if any failed, on a bad option, a missing
operand, or several targets and a last operand that is not a directory.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ln \-s /mnt/sata/1/notes notes
ln \-sf ../lib/new.lua lib.lua
ln \-s /bin/ls /bin/cat /tmp
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ls (1),
.BR rm (1),
.BR cp (1),
.BR mv (1)
